# interp.poke -- drive.poke, except the program does not name the interpreter.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES, and it cannot be proved by running it the ordinary way.
# bf/proc/drive.poke spawns "bfi" by name, which works only because the suite
# puts a file called bfi in the broker's working directory. That is the
# defect: the program is naming the interpreter it is ALREADY RUNNING UNDER,
# and the only name it can know is a literal in its own instruction stream.
# Start the broker with --interp pointing somewhere else and drive.poke goes
# on spawning bfi, silently and wrongly.
#
# This file sends a ZERO LENGTH PATH instead, which ABI.md section 7.15 gives
# the meaning "the interpreter the broker was launched with". The suite runs
# it under an interpreter that is NOT called bfi and is NOT in the working
# directory, so nothing in this file could name it and the spawn can only
# succeed through the substitution.
#
# IT ASKS FOR MINOR 1 IN THE HANDSHAKE, and that is the whole reason a minor
# version exists. Against a 1.0 broker a zero length path is INVAL, arriving
# from spawn in the middle of a conversation and reading like a malformed
# frame. Asking for 1.1 turns that into a clean VERSION refusal at the
# handshake, which is where a version problem belongs.
#
# The path is two bytes where the name was five. Rule Z pays here as it does
# everywhere: the correct spelling is also the cheapest one.
#
# Everything else is drive.poke. See it for the closes, which are the part
# that is easy to forget and impossible to debug, and for why the reads ask
# for one byte at a time.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 3, 7.10 to 7.16.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10, want major 1 and MINOR 1 -- see above.
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 01 00 00 00
READ 107
#   pipe  op 0e, flags 0.  Handles 4 (read) and 5 (write): the outer program
#   writes into 5 and the child reads from 4.
EMIT 0e 02 00 00 00
READ 11
#   pipe again.  Handles 6 (read) and 7 (write): the child writes into 7 and
#   the outer program reads from 6.
EMIT 0e 02 00 00 00
READ 11
#   spawn  op 0f, len 0x32 = 50 -- three less than drive.poke, which is the
#   difference between a two byte empty path and a five byte "bfi".
#     dir ffffffff meaning the broker's working directory, flags 0,
#     nfdmap 2, nargv 2, nenv 1, reserved 0
EMIT 0f 32 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 02 02 01 00
#     the descriptor map: child fd 0 gets handle 4, child fd 1 gets handle 7.
EMIT 00 04 00 00 00
EMIT 01 07 00 00 00
#     THE PATH, u16 prefixed, LENGTH ZERO: the interpreter the broker was
#     launched with.  There is no name here to be wrong.
EMIT 00 00
#     argv: "bfi", "echo.bf".  argv[0] is a LABEL and not a resolution -- it
#     is what the child sees in its own argv[0] and nothing looks it up -- so
#     it stays a literal, and the ABI does not reserve a value for it.
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 07 00 65 63 68 6f 2e 62 66
#     env: exactly one variable, "BFI_EOF=zero".  The environment is explicit
#     and never inherited, so this is the whole of the child's environment --
#     and echo.bf needs it, because the interpreter's default leaves the cell
#     unchanged at end of input and its loop would never terminate.
EMIT 0c 00 42 46 49 5f 45 4f 46 3d 7a 65 72 6f
#     the reply is a process handle: 8
READ 7
#   close the outer copies of the child's two ends.
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 07 00 00 00
READ 3
#   write  op 0b, handle 5, flags 0, "hi" -- into the child's stdin
EMIT 0b 08 00 05 00 00 00 00 00 68 69
READ 5
#   close handle 5, so the child sees end of input and stops
EMIT 0c 04 00 05 00 00 00
READ 3
#   read  op 0a, handle 6 -- the child's stdout -- ONE BYTE AT A TIME, for
#   the reason drive.poke sets out at length.
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 4
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 4
#   a third read: the child has exited and closed its end, so this is END
#   with an empty payload
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 3
#   wait  op 10, handle 8, flags 0 -- blocking.  state 1 exited, code 0.
EMIT 10 06 00 08 00 00 00 00 00
READ 7
#   close the pipe end and then the process handle.
EMIT 0c 04 00 06 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 08 00 00 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
